    {
      "selector": "section",
      "type": "content",
      "x": 0,
      "y": 1839,
      "width": 1920,
      "height": 400,
      "heading": "Sit the paper, then find out where the marks went",
      "backgroundColor": "#FFFFFF",
      "callsToAction": [],
      "text": "Sit the paper, then find out where the marks went Papers that behave like the exam A three-hour clock you cannot pause, an OMR-style navigator across all 200 questions, flag-for-review, and −0.25 applied to every wrong answer. The paper is handed in when time runs out whether or not your browser is open. A result you can act on Score and accuracy per subject and per topic, your rank and percentile against everyone who sat the same paper, your seconds-per-question against the 54-second budget, and the movement since your last attempt. Notes for the topics you drop marks on A study note for each",
      "layout": "centered",
      "assetUrls": []
    },
    {
      "selector": "section",
      "type": "content",
      "x": 384,
      "y": 2239,
      "width": 1152,
      "height": 494,
      "heading": "Something worth opening tomorrow",
      "backgroundColor": "#F7F8F9",
      "callsToAction": [],
      "text": "Something worth opening tomorrow The exam is months of small sessions, not one heroic weekend. Four of these run on their own once you start them. Daily quiz & streak Ten blueprint questions, the same ten for everyone, replaced every midnight in Kathmandu. Your streak and the day's top ten are on the dashboard. Ranked grand mocks Scheduled full papers everyone sits in the same hour, with a rank and percentile against the whole cohort when the window closes. A deck of your own mistakes Every question you get wrong joins a revision deck and comes back as flashcards until you answer it right. Get",
      "layout": "centered",
      "assetUrls": []
    },
    {
